// 9. Easy analogy: House

// Think of a class as a house.

//                 HOUSE
//         ┌───────────────────┐
//         │                   │
//         │  🔒 Private Room  │
//         │     speed         │
//         │                   │
//         │  🔒 Protected     │
//         │                   │
//         │  🚪 Public Door   │
//         │   accelerate()    │
//         │   brake()         │
//         └───────────────────┘
// Public

// Anyone with access to the object can use it.

// c.accelerate();
// Private

// Only code inside the class can access it.

// speed += 10;
// Protected

// Accessible inside the class and its child/derived classes.

// 10. A good interview answer

// If an interviewer asks:

// What are access specifiers?

// You can answer:

// Access specifiers control the accessibility of class members. public members can be accessed from anywhere through an object, private members can only be accessed inside the same class, and protected members can be accessed inside the class and derived classes.

// Why do we use private?

// We use private to protect an object's internal data and prevent invalid or unauthorized direct modification. Public member functions can provide controlled access and validation.

